11:Bind each teachable experience to the board/history, actor, candidate action, task horizon, and a complete independently replayable certificate. The certificate must cover the opponent replies required by the task's verification rule. Preserve its hash, verifier identity/version, checked status, proof scope, and resource result in the receipt. A search estimate, sampled weak reply, incomplete tree, timeout, or mismatched history is `unknown`; it sends no teaching event.
13:For a wrong selected move, provide a contrast when one is available: the verified consequence of the chosen action and a separately verified successful alternative from the same position. If a consequence depends on a later move, use only earlier action contexts that are independently bound to the complete certificate. Never credit a whole hypothetical line just because one displayed branch succeeds. Record whether the feedback addresses candidate omission, evaluation error, missed refutation, or search horizon; do not infer a diagnosis unsupported by the trace.
17:The certificate is teacher-side evidence, not a parameter update. Deliver the declared candidate/context cue and only the reinforcement activity permitted by the frozen, identity-checked neural interface. Receipts must distinguish the verified consequence from the simplified neural signal and document the timing, pathway, memory update, and unknown handling. This distinction prevents a valid teacher proof from being mistaken for biologically validated reward signaling.
19:Replay the exact same verified board/action/certificate sequence, order, and timing in absent, matched-shuffled, and disabled-plasticity controls. Keep every legal action available at evaluation. Independently audit certificates, pre-feedback rankings, delivered events, memory recurrence, and post-feedback all-legal rankings.
25:2026-10-08 implementation: the unfrozen runner now records a fresh ranking before each replay, binds it to the update's starting memory and verified certificate, and explicitly labels candidate-subset coverage and chosen/alternative reviews. Four adversarial fixture tests pass; the full suite passes 144/144. A control-name mismatch that would have delivered reinforcement to `no-channel` was corrected before any execution. The fixed experience schedule still requires a frozen protocol, matched runs, recovery validation and independent neural replay; no new graph experiment or learning result is implied. See the [implementation record](../../../research/journal/experiments/2026-10-08-feedback-quality-binding.md).
   1: # Current status
   2: 
   3: In the planned test, the researchers looked for settings that made the model rank a safe capture above every other move. They found settings for one of the three positions, short of the two required. After that test failed, a more extensive search found settings for all three positions. That later result is awaiting independent review. The model has yet to demonstrate that it can learn to pick safe captures and apply what it learned to unfamiliar positions.
   4: 
   5: The Fly Connections Memory investigation is paused while the researcher decides whether to request that review or close the work as a limited development result. See the [latest research handoff](next-checkpoint.md) for the decision and chronology.
   6: 
   7: ## Update: 2026-11-14
   8: 
   9: A three-board feedback test is built and checked, and its run awaits the researcher's approval; nothing has run on the real circuit. It asks whether lessons move the safe capture toward the top more than wrong lessons do, in a quieter version of the circuit. The activity scale for that circuit (0.014047) was checked against a replay of the earlier cell readings: on these boards it caps one cell on two moves in total, across two of the three boards. Codex reports that the full 6,046-connection recheck keeps at least 99% of every move difference. See the [protocol](design/neural-learning/three-board-feedback-test-2026-11-14.md) and the [scale check](../research/reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/calibration-replay-2026-11-14/README.md). The status below is unchanged.
  10: 
  11: ## Current status: 2026-11-09
  12: 
  13: **Volume 2, chapters 3 and 5 remain stopped pending the researcher's decision.** The revision-4 predeclared single-shot capacity gate on the widened 6,046-gain mask found one exact win out of three and failed. A later, stronger sequential exact search found settings on all three boards, but it was chosen after the gate failure and has not had independent review; it does not change the predeclared result. The earlier 2,096-gain result is a negative bound for a linear surrogate, not an exact-network impossibility proof. Reward-only and valence probes still left safe captures below their best rivals on all three boards. The reward-only probe included an absent arm; the valence probe was a single arm with baseline scores only. Neither had an independent audit. These are limited development diagnostics, not a registered full-run result.
  14: 
  15: No full revision-4 learning run is recorded, and the sealed test games remain unused. Properties 1-3 remain unmet; the project has no transferable-reasoning result. See the [November 9 experiment stop report](../research/reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/stop-report-2026-11-09.md), [checkpoint](next-checkpoint.md), and [dated journal](../research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md).
  16: 
  17: Earlier project updates and failures are preserved in the [post-restart history](current-status-history-2026-10-31-through-2026-11-09.md) and the [pre-restart status history](current-status-history-through-2026-10-30.md).

codex
Two interpretation gaps remain: the combination test ranks one move across two boards without requiring a useful move choice on either board, and failed interleaving is treated as evidence that no joint setting exists. Both leave weaker explanations open. The proposal already stops chess memory testing if acquisition fails; that safeguard is appropriate.

